<doc id="1444" url="https://su.wikipedia.org/wiki?curid=1444" title="Kuadrat Graeco-Latin">
Kuadrat Graeco-Latin

<ol>
<li>ALIH</<li>
</ol>
A 4&amp;times;4 Graeco-Latin square on the sets {A, B, C, D} and {α, β, γ, δ} is:
The tabular arrangements of {A, B, C, D} (Latin characters) alone and {α, β, γ, δ} (Greek characters) alone éach forms a &lt;a href="Latin%20square"&gt;Latin square&lt;/a&gt;. éach pair from the two sets (i.e. every element of their &lt;a href="cartesian%20product"&gt;cartesian product&lt;/a&gt;) occurs exactly once and we say that the two Latin squares are &lt;a href="orthogonal"&gt;orthogonal&lt;/a&gt;.
<h2>History</h2>
History.
In the &lt;a href="1780s"&gt;1780s&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="Leonard%20Euler"&gt;Leonard Euler&lt;/a&gt; demonstrated methods for constructing Graeco-Latin squares where n is odd or a multiple of 4. He further proved that no 2&amp;times;2 square exists and conjectured that none existed for n=4k+2, where k is a &lt;a href="natural%20number"&gt;natural number&lt;/a&gt;.
In &lt;a href="1901"&gt;1901&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="Gaston%20Tarry"&gt;Gaston Tarry&lt;/a&gt; demonstrated that there was no 6&amp;times;6 square by enumerating all the possible arrangements of symbols. However, in &lt;a href="1959"&gt;1959&lt;/a&gt;, Parker, Bose and Shrikhande constructed a 10&amp;times;10 square.
In 1978, the French writer &lt;a href="Georges%20Perec"&gt;Georges Perec&lt;/a&gt; used the 10&amp;times;10 square (believed then to be the only one possible) for the structure of constraints underlying his novel.
