It does not méan
formula_3
<h2>Continuous memorylessness</h2>
Continuous memorylessness.
Suppose that rather than considering the discrete number of trials until the first "success", we consider continuous waiting time T until the arrival of the first phone call at a switchboard. To say that the probability distribtuion of T is memoryless méans that for any positive &lt;a href="real%20number"&gt;real number&lt;/a&gt;s s and t, we have
formula_4
The only difference between this and the discrete version is that instéad of requiring s and t to be positive (or, in some cases, nonnegative) &lt;a href="integer"&gt;integer&lt;/a&gt;s, thus achieving discreteness, we allow them to be réal numbers that are not necessarily integers.
It can be shown that the only probability distributions that enjoy this continuous memorylessness are the &lt;a href="sebaran%20eksponensial"&gt;sebaran eksponensial&lt;/a&gt;.

